#: {{ux|en|The doctor put a '''cast''' on the boy’s broken arm.}}
# The mould used to make cast objects.
#: {{ux|en|A plaster '''cast''' was made from his face.}}
# {{lb|en|hawking}} The number of [[hawk]]s (or occasionally other birds) cast off at one time; a [[pair]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|passage=As when a '''cast''' of Faulcons make their flight / An an Herneshaw, that lyes aloft on wing {{...}}|6|7}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2007|author=w:Tim Blanning|title=The Pursuit of Glory|page=395|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2013
|passage=Louis XIV was keen, employing a total hawking personnel of 175 and adding a fourth '''cast''' of gyrfalcons to hunt hares in 1682 {{...}}.}}
# A [[squint]].
#* '''1847''', John Churchill, ''A manual of the principles and practice of ophthalmic medicine and surgery'', p. 389, [http://books.google.ru/books?id=ssXf87zIBjAC&lpg=PA389&ots=LC7EfevcXe&dq=cast%20in%20the%20eye&pg=PA389#v=onepage&q=1968&f=false paragraph 1968]:
#*: The image of the affected eye is clearer and in consequence the diplopy more striking the less the '''cast''' of the eye; hence the double vision will be noticed by the patient before the misdirection of the eye attracts the attention of those about him.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=Thomas Penn|title=Winter King|page=7|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2012
|passage=Arriving in Brittany, the Woodville exiles found a sallow young man, with dark hair curled in the shoulder-length fashion of the time and a penchant for expensively dyed black clothes, whose steady gaze was made more disconcerting by a '''cast''' in his left eye – such that while one eye looked at you, the other searched for you.}}
# [[visual|Visual]] [[appearance]].
#: {{ux|en|Her features had a delicate '''cast''' to them.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Photojournalism: The Professional's Approach| page=240| url=https://books.google.com/books?isbn=0240806107| author=Betsy Brill| year=2004| passage=Using a tungsten-balanced film outdoors results in a blue '''cast''' to the photo.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Australian's Housekeeper Bride| page=78| url=https://books.google.com/books?isbn=1426801130| author=Lindsay Armstrong| year=2007| passage=He stared down at his champagne glass with narrowed eyes and a hard '''cast''' to his mouth.}}
# The [[form]] of one's thoughts, mind etc.
#: a '''cast of mind''', a mental tendency.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1894|author=w:Wilson Lloyd Bevan|title=[[:s:Bevan - Sir William Petty (1894)/III#40|Sir William Petty : A Study in English Economic Literature]]|page=40
|passage=The '''cast of mind''' which prompted the plan was permanent, and in it are to be found both the strength and the weakness of Petty's character.}}
#* {{RQ:Lovecraft Cthulhu|passage=Young Wilcox’s rejoinder, which impressed my uncle enough to make him recall and record it verbatim, was of a fantastically poetic '''cast''' which must have typified his whole conversation, and which I have since found highly ​characteristic of him.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992|author=w:Hilary Mantel|title=A Place of Greater Safety|publisher=Harper Perennial|year_published=2007|page=330
|passage=I have read all her articles and come to admire both her elegant turn of phrase and the noble '''cast of mind''' which inspires it; but never, I confess, did I look to see beauty and wit so perfectly united.}}
# {{obs form|en|caste||hereditary social class of South Asia}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1821|title=Report of the American Board of Commissioners for Foreign Missions|volume=12-16|page=160
|passage=The brahmin's '''cast''' is higher than any other '''cast'''.}}
